WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.480
Hi! Happy Pizzamas!

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:03.680
It has been a tough few weeks.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:04.940
I mean, it’s been a tough year.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.540
Even for my cat, and she doesn’t really
understand celebrity deaths or politics.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.080
She is more of a philosophy and golf kind
of gal.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:11.840
My name is Lex.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.220
I have a YouTube channel, but more importantly,
I have this cat.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:18.980
Her name is Felicity, she’s been an indoor
cat all her life, she is nine years old, and

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:20.699
I adopted her about a month ago.

00:00:20.699 --> 00:00:24.030
She’s very friendly, she likes being stroked
and sitting on laps, and the other night at

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:29.670
about 3am, she decided to scoot her butt across
my bed sheet, I guess it was itchy, and then

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.430
I forgot, and I slept on it.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:32.250
The sheet.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:33.510
Not her butthole.

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:37.000
But at this point, in terms of hygiene, there’s
basically no difference.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.140
There are a lot of great things about owning
a cat.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:40.640
Great for me. Selfish.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.860
She gives me a sense of routine and purpose.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:46.660
She makes me responsible for someone other
than myself, which in turn makes me more responsible

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:50.250
for myself, because I have to remember to
do things like clean up my living space and

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.530
not leave old food just on the ground.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:53.690
She really relaxes me.

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:58.330
A cat is basically like a furry hot water
bottle with a face.

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:00.460
If you picture that literally, it’s not
very relaxing.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.920
I mean, also, she’s just so cute, oh my
God, it’s physically painful, please make

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:04.960
her leave the room.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:06.760
My cat gets stuff out of this, too.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.000
Food, shelter, water: the power trio.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.760
Catnip, so, regular access to legal highs,
and an owner, who, like the parent of a newborn

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.220
(how’s it going, Hank?) has committed to
the fact that they will not get a good night’s

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:18.939
sleep for a very long time.

00:01:18.939 --> 00:01:22.059
So I don’t mind that she likes to be the
little spoon, or that she wakes me up in the

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:23.040
middle of the night.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.680
Although she has developed this great trick
where she reaches out at about 2am and punches

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:29.160
me in the face to try and get me to wake up
and stroke her.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.159
It’s so adorable.

00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:34.750
The more I think about it, though, the more
it’s weird that I’ve brought this animal

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:35.710
into my home.

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:39.850
She’s domesticated, obviously, but I don’t
think that my bedroom is necessarily her natural

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:40.350
habitat.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:45.320
I really don’t want to delve into the horrifying
depths of the indoor vs. outdoor cat conversation.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:46.340
People yell there.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:50.060
As Felic has never been outside, she’s a
bit more senior, she would be in a lot of

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.510
trouble if I just opened the front door and
said, “Go!

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:54.010
Run! Be free, my child!

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:55.880
Good luck with the big metal dogs with wheels.”

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:58.729
If they’ve never known anything different,
do you think they’re just like, “Cool,

00:01:58.729 --> 00:01:59.509
this is the world.

00:01:59.509 --> 00:02:02.719
I’m gonna eat some treats given to me by
the big stupid talking noodle.”

00:02:02.719 --> 00:02:07.320
Or does my cat look at the very large tall climbing
tree that I bought her and think, like,

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.140
"What—what are you trying to pull here? That is not a tree."

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:13.060
Something I do find interesting is the difference
in lifespan.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:16.280
I looked up the lifespan of a rabbit, and
it said eight to twelve years domesticated,

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:17.740
one to two years in the wild.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:21.940
In the wild, animals are fighting for food,
mates, survival, sometimes it’s cold, they

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:25.540
can have untreated illnesses and injuries,
predators are always giving them heck, nobody

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:28.300
lets them eat a small bit of cheese if they’ve
been good that day.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:31.840
Is it better to have a long, comfortable life
that’s not necessarily instinctive to you,

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:36.280
or to have a short, natural one where you
run free, terrified, with no idea what cheese even is?

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:41.160
I am, of course, just talking about domesticated
animals, obviously, not like orcas in a tank.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.320
I guess you could say that humans weren’t
really supposed to live inside, though.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:46.940
But someone what like, “Hey, I put a rug
down in this cave.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.750
You should come in here ‘cause it’s, um,
it’s better."

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:50.920
Someone fact-check that for me.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.500
A big problem that’s come from our love
of pets is overbreeding.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:57.840
We are creating a huge overpopulation problem
that often leads to animals being without homes.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.300
That’s why animal charities always encourage
you to spay and neuter your pets, and to adopt

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:03.430
rather than shop if you’re thinking of getting
an animal.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:07.650
People think rescue animals are so damaged
and difficult, but Felic is not damaged or difficult.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:11.480
Although I have taught her to high five, but
she refuses to distinguish between the high

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:12.840
five and the low five.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.860
That’s the same thing, Felic. It's the same.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:18.480
I think my cat is very happy here with me,
and I can’t go back in time and undo the

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.840
domestication of pets, so I am happy to give
one a loving home.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.620
Who was the first person to look at animals,
especially like wild cats and wolves, and go,

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:29.520
“Yeah, I want that guy licking yogurt
off my hand.”

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.220
I don’t have the answers to any of these
questions.

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:31.980
Maybe you do.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:35.520
Thank you for joining me here on vlogbrothers
during Pizzamas, and for sticking with me

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.950
when I decided I just wanted to make a video
about cats.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:41.470
I’ve been on the World Wide Web for a while,
I know what you kids like.

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:43.590
John, I’ll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:44.800
I’ve always wanted to say that.

